+`lab/e2e-upgrade-release.sh` is the upgrade between real releases (#644) on `lab/docker-compose-upgrade-release.yml` (the FRR edge of `lab/frr` and one Packeteer container) with `lab/packeteer-upgrade-release.yaml` (observe, sqlite, sign-in on). It runs the v1.12.0 image, pinned by digest in the script (`PREV_IMAGE` must contain `@sha256:`), and writes a second user, a pin, a pause, and an acknowledged `provider.down` alert. It stops that container and starts the image built from this tree on the same `packeteer-data` volume (named per compose project, so a local run cannot touch a real one), and checks that `-check` passes, that the pin, pause, acknowledgement, users, event history, and audit log are restored, that the mode is still observe, that no `steering.lost` fired, and that the edge holds no route from Packeteer. Then it does the downgrade: the v1.12.0 image started again on the volume shows the same state (no config key is newer than that release, so none is removed; the README upgrade notes say to remove such keys first). Container exit codes are 0 on SIGTERM. It complements `lab/e2e-upgrade.sh`, which upgrades a build of this tree against a fake release server. CI runs it as the `upgrade-release` job.
 
 `lab/e2e-registry.sh` is registry hygiene (#289) against in-lab registries only (#657) on its own topology (`lab/docker-compose-registry.yml`): the RPKI-invalid lab's edge and transit-a (transit-a sends `203.0.113.0/24` and `203.0.113.128/25` from AS64501), Packeteer in observe (`lab/packeteer-registry.yaml`), and `registry-stub` (`lab/registrystub`, 192.0.2.40), a stand-in for a whois IRR, a VRP JSON document, PeeringDB, and the RIPE REST API. The lab network is `internal`, so no live RADB, ARIN, RIPE, PeeringDB, or other registry is reachable; the script checks that. It checks: (1) the first scan reports exactly a missing route object, a stale route object, a ROA gap (maxLength), a PeeringDB max-prefix drift, and a port-speed drift, with their diffs. (2) The scan only reads the stand-in. (3) A wrong digest, a ROA finding, and the stale delete (offered only after a second scan) change nothing. (3a, #673) The lab config scans every 3m, so the second scan runs: the delete is then offered, a wrong digest is refused and the object stays, the shown digest deletes it on the stand-in only, and the delete and the refusal are in the audit log without a credential.
 
